    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP on this July 6th, the daily efficiency model highlights **James Wood** and **Corbin Carroll** as our primary engines for production. **James Wood** earns an elite 76.14 score, leveraging a high-leverage leadoff spot and a favorable matchup against Burrows. Similarly, **Corbin Carroll** sits comfortably at 72.02 against Buehler; his elite speed profile remains our anchor for the scarce SB category, and the dynamic platoon edge against a righty keeps his ceiling high despite a slight park penalty. **Isaac Paredes** also merits a strong start (68.21) against Mikolas, benefiting from an inflated SP skill adjustment and a favorable batting order, making him our most reliable run producer today.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved balancing the surging **Austin Riley** against the scarcity of our speed-first strategy. While **Austin Riley** carries a 59.3 score\u2014just shy of our \"Strong\" tier\u2014he remains a risky start due to a 34% rain risk in his matchup against Peralta. We are proceeding with **Austin Riley** in the UTIL slot for now, but he remains the first candidate to swap if weather worsens. At the bottom of our start threshold, **Dalton Rushing** (41.13) and **Jose Caballero** (45.22) serve as our pivots. **Jose Caballero** is particularly suppressed today by a heavy platoon penalty; however, we are forced to weather that drag because our organizational depth, including **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Max Muncy**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Alex Freeland**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Edwin Arroyo**, **Cole Young**, and **Henry Bolte**, offers no superior alternatives for today's specific slate. We remain particularly thin at the middle infield and catcher positions, requiring us to ride **Brice Turang** (55.58) despite his low-end efficiency score due to the high probability of a stolen base opportunity against May.",
